Date: Fri, 12 Mar 93 14:55:53 EST
From: eisrael@suneast.East.Sun.COM (Elias Israel - SunSelect Engineering)
Subject: META: Is an armed society a polite society?

Robin Hanson writes:
> What if we "arm" everyone on the list, giving them the right to
> "shoot" any one person on the list, if they also get "shot" back.

This experiment has been tried, but with disappointing results. On many
of the early MUDs (Multi-User Dungeons) that were popular for a time on
the Internet, it was possible to "kill" another player for a small
investment in gold. The killed player would be removed from the game
temporarily (until the user could log back in) and would be moved to a
"limbo" location and possibly stripped of posessions.

Eventually, killing people for no reason was considered impolite, but
the ability to kill did not really produce polite behaviour in general.
While being killed was annoying, it wasn't enough punishment for the
threat of it to stop someone in other annoying behaviours. Killing
someone whose behaviour you didn't like was more likely to elicit
retaliation than a cessation of the offensive behaviour.

In addition, some players ran around killing others for the pure fun
of annoying them. Some only killed certain kinds of players. Robots
were a frequent target because early robots were rather stupid and
not much fun to talk to. (I personally designed a robot that had
a very strict tit-for-tat strategy. It killed only those who'd killed
it before, and only as many times as it had been wronged.)

Ultimately, the ability to kill players evolved out of the later MUDs
or was subject to strict rules of engagement (e.g. safe houses) so
as to limit the ill-feeling that the free-for-all killing had lead to.

I don't think that any "armed society" that we could create here would
overcome the problem that the MUDs discovered: unless killing is
a significant, unalterable punishment that can be meted out and can
be taken seriously, it will degenerate into an annoyance tactic, no
different from any other kind of flaming. And I doubt that we really
want to convert the list into some kind of ASCII duel ("See Mel
Gibson in: _List Warriors!_") just to try it out.

Elias Israel
eisrael@east.sun.com

