Date: Fri, 12 Mar 93 15:22:28 EST
From: Brian.Hawthorne@East.Sun.COM (Brian Holt Hawthorne - SunSelect Engineering)
Subject: PHIL: Drive to reproduce

Alan Barksdale:
This model suggests that we'd have less of a self-genociding problem among
high-tech populations if the act of having a child was in itself an inborn
desire.

Me:
In fact, nearly any woman who has borne a child, and most anyone who has 
participated in a birth, will tell you that it is absolutely astounding
that anyone would ever go through it again (although there are a very
few births that are not like running a marathon and having major surgery
all at once).

So, why do women have more than one birth? An informal survey of my
wife and friends who have been through it indicates that there is already
some sort of bizarre instinctive behaviour going on to suppress the
memory of the difficult parts of the birth. Every one of them reports
that immediately following birth, sometimes as soon as the next day,
they were saying, "It wasn't that bad, I could do it again". By a year 
or two later, many of them have forgotten it sufficiently to actually
initiate the process again! (Like my wife and I...) When they rationally
sit down and go through the events of birth, they do in fact remember
what it was like, and wonder if they are crazy.

I don't know if this counts as an inborn desire to have children, but
it certainly seems like some sort of innate mechanism for decreasing the
desire to avoid it...

