Date: Fri, 12 Mar 93 14:55:09 CST
From: derek@cs.wisc.edu (Derek Zahn)
Subject: PHIL:  Extropian magick?

Scott DeLancey makes the point that 

> manipulating, controlling, altering, improving one's consciousness

is an Extropian activity.  So far, so good, so long as we don't
push that slippery term "consciousness" very far [but that's
a debate for another time].  He also says that

> the interesting claims of yoga, various meditiation systems, magick, etc.,

provide concrete ways of achieving these extropian ends, and
that the oft-associated supernatural sideshows often associated
with those areas of study are not the whole story.

No problem!  I don't think that anybody on the list would have much
argument with experience-based reports of how meditation can help
concentration or how yoga or tai ch'i can perhaps enhance perception
of one's surroundings, or whatever.  Nobody has complained about
such claims (largely because nobody has discussed such things).
I for one would welcome such material -- but remember, just as
your hypothetical mediaeval scholar had to have some criteria
for separating the alchemical wheat from the chaff, so do we
regarding consciousness.  What could the criteria be, except
either direct experience (and unfortunately we don't have the
time to test every subjective claim this way), or reasoned
discourse?

Let's get specific:  You mention 'magickal experimentation'
as one of your reasonable examples.  I choose that one because
I haven't a clue what 'magick' is.  Your scholar is apparently
to hope to be lucky enough to find

> a few people making systematic observations
> that eventually might actually contribute to the basis of a science.

The implication is that this would apply to your 'magick' as
well.  Could you clue me in a bit (I don't expect a detailed
research paper, just some general hints) on what "systematic
observations" have been done with 'magick' that we should
pay attention to it?

The problem is that the world is full of wild claims (often
contradictory).  Why should we prefer one set of claims over
another?  How are we able to evaluate claims of flying yogis
or flying consciousnesses without either reasoned discourse
or concrete demonstration?

derek

