Date: Fri, 12 Mar 93 16:07:05 EST
From: drw@BOURBAKI.MIT.EDU
Subject: PPL: extracting compensation

   From: pmetzger@snark.shearson.com (Perry E. Metzger)

   People like you seem
   to want to hold a PPL system to a higher standard than the existing
   system.

Of course.  Otherwise, switching to it would not be an improvement.

   We must compare apples to apples. Indeed, as a concession to the fact
   that PPL systems are difficult to study in the absense of real complete
   ones, I generally stipulate the worst in a PPL system and then compare it
   not against the worst abuses of government but merely the average everyday
   ones, and PPL systems still seem to come out ahead.

Except that no-one seems to have given a convincing demonstration that
a PPL is stable, at least in something resembling modern, industrial
society.  There seem to be lots of "private law" systems in modern
society, but none of them are really involved in maintaining the
public peace.  Usually, they merely regulate the relationships of a
more or less homogeneous social or professional group.  There seem to
have been a few PPLs (or semi-PPLs) in "history", that is, cultures
that wrote, but they seem to have been in rather static, homogeneous
cultures.

You want to do away with government, but the examples that spring to
mind are Somalia and Bosnia.  That may not be fair, but the real,
underlying question is actually fair: What evidence can be presented
that a PPL can effectively keep the bandits under control (at least as
well as governments do)?  That is, without having to cede so much
power to the PPL that it functions for all practical purposes as a
government does.

Or, to be blunt, there is a natural suspicion that the lack of "real
complete" examples of PPLs may be because one cannot be constructed.

The best argument would be to construct a PPL-based community.  There
would be a certain amount of resistance from the surrounding state(s),
but that wouldn't be too intense to make success impossible, providing
the PPL didn't annoy the neighbors too much.  The U.S. has had a
plethora of utopian communities of many different sorts, and a
considerable number of them still survive.  (Some of them even openly
violate the laws -- the neo-Yoruba community in North Carolina, and
the Mormon communities of southern Utah both practice polygamy, with
no real interference from The Authorities.)  In fact, the history of
these communities would be a good place to discover what sorts of
social arrangements work and which ones don't.

(Of course, this argument is really religious.  As you've stated
before, you demand that governments be abolished whether or not that
brings about an improvement in the quality of life.  I, on the other
hand, don't object to governments.  Oh, well.)

Dale

