Date: Fri, 12 Mar 1993 11:56:57 -0800 (PST)
From: Scott C DeLancey <delancey@darkwing.uoregon.edu>
Subject: PHIL:  Extropian magick?

      This is obviously inspired by the PHIL/META argument involving
Tim May, Eric Raymond, and others, but is intended to be orthogonal
enough to some of the main issues there that I've retitled it.  The
general pro-scientific, anti-mystical mush attitude informing a lot
of the debate (which I'm generally in sympathy with) seems occasionally
to veer toward a general condemnation of a set of "mystical" practices
and disciplines which I think is unwise.  (When I use "you" in a
personal sense here I guess I have in mind Tim and Ray, but if I
seem to be imputing ideas to you that you don't want don't take it
personally).
     First, I take it that it is quite consistent with the idea of
"boundless expansion" and general Extropian attitudes to be interested
in the possibilities of manipulating, controlling, altering, improving 
one's consciousness, as much as one's body.  (Cf. the recent "self-help"
book thread).  Science and technology ahve now reached the point where
we can think quite seriously about pretty radical alteration and
manipulation of the body.  But the various sciences concerned with
consciousness are nowhere near the point of being able to provide any
concrete guidance along these lines.  (Read Dennett's _Consciousness
Explained_, or Churchland's _Neurophilosophy_, for a more-or-less
state-of-the-art summary of what we might know--if your imagination
inclines that way you may get some vague ideas about what a technology
of consciousness might have to contend with, but I don't see anything
in that literature that will tell you how to do anything, or even what
might someday be doable).
     Now, the interesting claims of yoga, various meditiation systems,
magick, etc., (as well as, to some extent, martial arts) involve just this. 
True, there are lots of (IMO) uninteresting claims floating around all of
these disciplines concerning acquisition of supernatural powers, contact
with supernatural entities, and other nonsense.  But, contrary to widespread 
belief, these silly claims are not integral to all of the systems that they
are sometimes made for.  That is, there are many teachers and practitioners 
of yoga, magick, etc., whose interest is entirely in investigating phenomena
of consciousness, and who take the claims of flying yogis no more seriously
than you do.
     Here's an analogy.  Imagine yourself an aspiring young rationalist
in Europe in 1475. You want to understand substances, and matter, and the 
kind of stuff we would now call chemistry.  What should you do?  Obviously
the only available route that leads in any direction close to what you
want is to study alchemy.  (Yes, for everybody who will want to explain
it to me, I know that that's not what alchemy is Really About, but
let's leave that for the time being).  And what will you find if
you do that?  Armies of frauds and mountebanks, out to get ahold
of other people's money.  Buckets of fatuous idiots, dreaming hopelessly
of eternal life* and happiness if they only find the right formula.
But also, with luck, a few people making systematic observations
that eventually might actually contribute to the basis of a science.
These were probably few and far between, and you might never be
lucky enough to bump into any--but if you did find any, it would be
among the alchemists; no sense at all bothering to look anywhere else.
     Well, among the modern alchemists of consciousness it's easy to
find the frauds and the fatheads.  Whether there is any equivalent
to the third category is obviously open to debate (since we're debating
it)--but I think it's an entirely defensible proposition that if you 
hope to find people who can teach you anything practical about consciousness,
that is where you're going to have to look.  In any case it isn't the case
that yoga, or meditation, or Aikido, or for that matter magickal
experimentation, are necessarily or intrinsically religious, or
necessarily have any truck with "spirits".  (OK, you can't study
Aikido or Tai Ch'i without thinking in terms of ki/qi/ch'i, but you
can if you want to think in terms of it without "believing" in it
in the way that you believe in, say, electricity). 
     This is certainly not to say that you, or anyone else, *should*
be interested in any of this, or take it seriously, any more than we
should expect that everyone should take cryonics seriously.  (I hope we
don't expect that, because I, for one, simply can't).  You may say that my
hypothetical young rationalist should give up and devote his energies to 
studying things that can be intelligently studied in his era.  Well, we 
could hardly criticize anyone for making that choice, but if he's really 
interested in the other, how Extropian is it to tell him to shut up and be
satisfied with what's already available?


*Of course THOSE people WERE idiots :-). 

Scott DeLancey




